###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# On the same CPU setup the Orb teacher needs 65.39 s for the 1,000 He-benzene configurations (about 5,030 microseconds per atom) and the student 2.34 s (about 28 times faster); the student has 4.25e5 parameters vs about 2.55e7 for Orb (Sec. IV A). The data-efficiency gain corresponds to about 5e5 CPU hours of CCSD(T) data generation; one SAPT single point costs 21.08 CPU hours.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - Errors are in cm-1 or cm-1/atom (no schema unit; recorded with unit null). The 'after FT' column of Table S2 uses the same CCSD(T) fine-tuning protocol for each teacher; the role is validation, as stated for Tables I, II and S2.
# - The PAH errors are measured only along one-dimensional cuts whose relation to the training data is not stated, so their role is null. Teacher models are named by label only (base_model) and not linked to registry models; maximum errors (Table S2) are not schema metrics. The adaptive SAPT cutoff is treated as a cutoff scheme of SOG-Net (a setting), as for dynamic cutoffs in METHODS.md.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: datacite).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/shen2026.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

